%[text] %[text:anchor:T_EC7F2924] # Two-dimensional biquadratic basis functions
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] Construction of the biquadratic two-dimensional basis functions using a tensor product of the one-dimensional quadratic [Lagrange polynomials](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lagrange_polynomial).
%[text] 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_5A5D8497] Go to [THIS](file:.\main_Chapter1_BasisFunctions.m) Section in the main driver script *Two-dimensional biquadratic basis functions*
%[text] Go to [PREVIOUS](file:.\main_Chapter13_TwoDimensionalBilinearBasisFunctions.m) Section *Two-dimensional bilinear basis functions*
%[text] 
%[text] **Author: Dr.-Ing. Andreas Apostolatos**
%[text] **Date: 10.06.2026**
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F203EBCC] ## Preamble
vars = "mshQ2";
str = "This m-file should be ran from parent m-file " + ...
    "main_Chapter1_BasisFunctions.m";
for ii = 1:length(vars)
    if ~exist(vars(ii), 'var') 
        error(strcat("Variable ", vars(ii), " is not defined. ", str));
    end
end
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_1DD2A62E] ## Definition of the biquadratic Lagrange Polynomials
%[text] The definition of the biquadratic Lagrange Polynomials is similar to the definition of the bilinear Lagrange polynomials with the only difference that the tensor product contruction rule involves the one-dimensional quadratic Lagrange polynomials.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![biquadratic_mesh.png](text:image:3e43)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_73D49D21] ## Set-up of the two-dimensional biquadratic Lagrange polynomials in physical space
%[text] The construction of the biquadratic Lagrange polynomials is similar to the construction of the bilinear Lagrange polynomials, namely:
%[text] $l\_{i,j}^{\\text{Q}\_2}(X, Y) = \\prod\_{1 \\le m \\le 3, m \\neq j} \\frac{X - X\_m}{X\_i - X\_m} \\prod\_{1 \\le n \\le 3, n \\neq j} \\frac{Y - Y\_n}{Y\_i - Y\_n}$
%[text] ![](text:image:0f6f) **Reflect**
%[text] Can the latter rule be used to construct elements that have different polynomial degrees along each Cartesian direction?
%[text] ![](text:image:2c06) **Reflect**
%[text] What is the challenge for such elements that attain different polynomial orders along different directions?
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_4768E100] ### Initialize auxiliary variable
ijQ2 = ["1,1", "1,3", "3,3", "3,1", "1,2", "2,3", "3,2", "2,1", "2,2"];
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_C786536E] ### Set-up a plot to display all the Lagrange Polynomials associated with the selected element
elQ2 = 3;
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_1461B058] ### Plot the biaquadratic polynomials on the physical space
plot2dBiquadraticLagrangePolynomialsOnPhysicalSpace ... %[output:group:23a2ef09] %[output:3e4512a0]
    (elQ2, mshQ2, numVertices, l1L2, l2L2, l3L2, ijQ2); %[output:group:23a2ef09] %[output:3e4512a0]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_92AB4ED1] ## Two-dimensional biquadratic Lagrange Polynomials
%[text] As for the bilinear quadrilateral elements, also for the biquadratic bilinear elements it is chosen the parametric space $(\\xi,\\eta) \\in \[-1, 1\]^2$. Similar to the parametric space of the quadratic elements, one needs to place additional nodes in the parametric space to allow for interpolating a biquadratic function by means of the high-order interpolatory Lagrange polynomials.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![biquadratic_mesh.png](text:image:3c8d)
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_B96DD519] ### Set-up of the two-dimensional biquadratic Lagrange Polynomials in the parametric space
syms l11Q2(xi, eta) l13Q2(xi, eta) l33Q2(xi, eta) l31Q2(xi, eta) ...
    l12Q2(xi, eta) l23Q2(xi, eta) l32Q2(xi, eta) l21Q2(xi, eta) l22Q2(xi, eta)
l11Q2(xi, eta) = l1L2(xi, -1, 0, 1)*l1L2(eta, -1, 0, 1);
l13Q2(xi, eta) = l1L2(xi, -1, 0, 1)*l3L2(eta, -1, 0, 1);
l33Q2(xi, eta) = l3L2(xi, -1, 0, 1)*l3L2(eta, -1, 0, 1);
l31Q2(xi, eta) = l3L2(xi, -1, 0, 1)*l1L2(eta, -1, 0, 1);
l12Q2(xi, eta) = l1L2(xi, -1, 0, 1)*l2L2(eta, -1, 0, 1);
l23Q2(xi, eta) = l3L2(xi, -1, 0, 1)*l2L2(eta, -1, 0, 1);
l32Q2(xi, eta) = l2L2(xi, -1, 0, 1)*l1L2(eta, -1, 0, 1);
l21Q2(xi, eta) = l2L2(xi, -1, 0, 1)*l3L2(eta, -1, 0, 1);
l22Q2(xi, eta) = l2L2(xi, -1, 0, 1)*l2L2(eta, -1, 0, 1);
lijQ2 = {l11Q2, l13Q2, l33Q2, l31Q2, l12Q2, l23Q2, l32Q2, l21Q2, l22Q2};
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_37C70B5F] ### Visualization of the two-dimensional biquadratic Lagrange Polynomials in the parametric space
t3 = tiledlayout(3, 3); %[output:5451d227]
for ii = 1:numel(lijQ2)
    nexttile %[output:5451d227]
    fsurf(lijQ2{ii}, [-1 1 -1 1], "FaceColor", [217, 218, 219]./255, ...
        "FaceAlpha", 0.5);
    xlabel("\xi")
    ylabel("\eta")
    zlabel(sprintf("l_{%s}", num2str(ijQ2(ii))))
end
title(t3, "Biquadratic Lagrange Polynomials"); %[output:5451d227]

